1040
00:58:29.745 --> 00:58:33.196
All right. Mayor, Mayor Pro Tem, Council members, thank you for having me today.

1041
00:58:33.816 --> 00:58:36.876
I want to talk about the downtown Salisbury parking study.

1042
00:58:39.036 --> 00:58:42.996
So starting off, we looked at the study to really think about what are the goals

1043
00:58:43.016 --> 00:58:46.916
and the purpose of this plan, and that's really to address how

1044
00:58:46.996 --> 00:58:50.736
downtown Salisbury is going to grow over time and how we could support economic

1045
00:58:50.776 --> 00:58:54.596
development, enhance the parking and transportation experience, and really

1046
00:58:54.636 --> 00:58:58.596
support businesses and people as they move, park, and connect throughout the

1047
00:58:58.656 --> 00:58:59.316
study area.

1048
00:59:00.156 --> 00:59:03.696
What we did was we analyzed the parking supply and demand within the study

1049
00:59:03.836 --> 00:59:07.706
area. We looked at how that supply could be used to support that

1050
00:59:07.736 --> 00:59:11.536
economic development, looked at the growth plan over the next 10

1051
00:59:11.596 --> 00:59:14.876
years, and then also took a look at how we could integrate

1052
00:59:14.936 --> 00:59:18.456
multimodal travel throughout the area.

1053
00:59:20.216 --> 00:59:23.816
The primary goal is to revitalize Main Street and unlock that economic

1054
00:59:23.836 --> 00:59:27.625
development. And as we know, parking can be a big part of how people

1055
00:59:27.676 --> 00:59:31.596
interact with an area. It's the first and last experience typically, so

1056
00:59:31.656 --> 00:59:33.936
having a good experience is really important to that.

1057
00:59:34.776 --> 00:59:38.036
And we identified seven planning priorities throughout the process.

1058
00:59:39.016 --> 00:59:41.876
Maintain the Main Street character while supporting growth,

1059
00:59:43.076 --> 00:59:45.096
promoting infill and adaptive reuse,

1060
00:59:47.336 --> 00:59:50.976
improving connectivity to amenities and key investments in the area,

1061
00:59:51.976 --> 00:59:55.956
reestablishing downtown as a safe, walkable, and accessible destination,

1062
00:59:57.176 --> 01:00:00.496
creating a business-friendly environment that's inviting to customers,

1063
01:00:01.066 --> 01:00:04.786
encouraging exploration through enhanced mobility and a park once

1064
01:00:04.856 --> 01:00:08.776
experience, which is not having to move your car between one location and the

1065
01:00:08.796 --> 01:00:12.416
next. You can actually park and walk around and enjoy the entire

1066
01:00:12.516 --> 01:00:16.496
area, go back to your car when you're leaving, and then open that space

1067
01:00:16.576 --> 01:00:17.515
up for someone else.

1068
01:00:18.356 --> 01:00:21.196
And then lastly, support businesses and event activation.

1069
01:00:21.516 --> 01:00:24.876
We know that there's a lot of events that occur in the downtown area,

1070
01:00:25.296 --> 01:00:28.756
making sure that those events can really thrive and business can thrive as

1071
01:00:28.816 --> 01:00:29.776
well.

1072
01:00:30.976 --> 01:00:34.836
We evaluated this over a three-phased process, and that first phase was really

1073
01:00:34.876 --> 01:00:38.766
focused on data collection, getting a good understanding of what

1074
01:00:38.776 --> 01:00:42.716
parking is provided within the area, how that parking is being utilized,

1075
01:00:43.336 --> 01:00:46.776
and then any of the boundaries or barriers to access.

1076
01:00:47.556 --> 01:00:51.426
We did a pretty extensive engagement process where we did both online and

1077
01:00:51.456 --> 01:00:55.176
in-person meetings to get to know what people thought about the

1078
01:00:55.256 --> 01:00:59.056
area and their experience. Then we analyzed all of that information to

1079
01:00:59.076 --> 01:01:02.996
develop our recommendations. So it was very much a data-driven process

1080
01:01:03.036 --> 01:01:07.016
that was also fueled by community engagement and good

1081
01:01:07.196 --> 01:01:08.416
responses through a survey.

1082
01:01:10.226 --> 01:01:13.316
So what we saw, this is the study area that you see here.

1083
01:01:14.076 --> 01:01:17.836
Within this area, the on-street parking spaces are in orange, and then the

1084
01:01:17.896 --> 01:01:21.455
off-street parking spaces, parking in surface lots, are in blue.

1085
01:01:21.856 --> 01:01:25.826
And what we saw was there is just about 1,000 spaces within the study area,

1086
01:01:26.456 --> 01:01:28.196
and it was pretty split half and half

1087
01:01:29.272 --> 01:01:31.092
We looked at parking utilization

1088
01:01:32.052 --> 01:01:35.632
on a Thursday in March, and what we saw was that

1089
01:01:36.052 --> 01:01:39.772
at its peak use, the parking system here was only used about

1090
01:01:39.992 --> 01:01:43.362
52%. So there was quite a bit of spaces that were being

1091
01:01:43.412 --> 01:01:45.992
unused. That was at that noon timeframe.

1092
01:01:47.292 --> 01:01:51.252
We also looked and saw that during event conditions in the evening,

1093
01:01:51.622 --> 01:01:55.332
the parking was still about 72% utilized, with

1094
01:01:55.412 --> 01:01:58.992
areas like the city parking lot only being about 50% to 70%

1095
01:01:59.112 --> 01:02:02.772
utilized. So there were a good amount of spaces that were going unused within the

1096
01:02:02.832 --> 01:02:03.512
study area.

1097
01:02:04.542 --> 01:02:08.022
And we did look at this, the event during the Wine About

1098
01:02:08.032 --> 01:02:11.632
Winter, that was on a Friday evening, seeing that there were

1099
01:02:12.752 --> 01:02:16.332
areas of very high demand, but there was still a lot of supply that was not being

1100
01:02:16.412 --> 01:02:17.732
leveraged to its maximum.

1101
01:02:19.552 --> 01:02:23.422
We also looked at future development, and what we did was we met with developers,

1102
01:02:23.492 --> 01:02:27.272
we talked with the cities for all of the planned developments, and we identified

1103
01:02:27.692 --> 01:02:31.672
about 13,000 square feet of recreational community space

1104
01:02:31.692 --> 01:02:35.422
that we modeled out, about just over 10,000 square feet of

1105
01:02:35.612 --> 01:02:39.372
office, 8,000 square feet of food and beverage restaurant

1106
01:02:39.412 --> 01:02:42.992
spaces, around 5,000 square feet of retail, a

1107
01:02:43.112 --> 01:02:47.072
33-key hotel, and then 45 residential units, all

1108
01:02:47.132 --> 01:02:51.092
planned to be developed over the next 10 years, or potentially being developed

1109
01:02:51.112 --> 01:02:54.612
over the next 10 years. So with that development, there comes the

1110
01:02:54.672 --> 01:02:58.592
additional parking demand that we evaluated overlaid

1111
01:02:58.792 --> 01:03:00.512
on our existing demand.

1112
01:03:02.032 --> 01:03:05.532
With that, we modeled that within the next year, we're looking at an

1113
01:03:05.592 --> 01:03:09.512
additional demand of about 36 parking spaces that we need

1114
01:03:09.572 --> 01:03:13.372
to be able to accommodate. Over the five-year period, we're looking at

1115
01:03:13.452 --> 01:03:16.952
179 spaces, and then over 10 years, we're up to

1116
01:03:17.172 --> 01:03:21.152
350 spaces. So definitely increasing demand as

1117
01:03:21.192 --> 01:03:22.812
the area continues to develop.

1118
01:03:23.952 --> 01:03:25.972
Now, what does that do to our baseline?

1119
01:03:26.452 --> 01:03:29.512
That takes our baseline that we would have during that typical day

1120
01:03:30.552 --> 01:03:34.252
from that 52% up to around that 77%.

1121
01:03:35.632 --> 01:03:39.391
And during events, we're looking at a parking demand going up well

1122
01:03:39.472 --> 01:03:43.412
over 100% of what we have, so actually exceeding our

1123
01:03:43.452 --> 01:03:47.232
existing parking supply. Now, this is again spurred by a lot of growth,

1124
01:03:47.332 --> 01:03:51.112
spurred by events. As that continues to develop, there's a need

1125
01:03:51.212 --> 01:03:54.312
for additional infrastructure and management of the system.

1126
01:03:56.992 --> 01:04:00.832
So as a part of our community engagement process, we received over 350

1127
01:04:00.892 --> 01:04:04.572
online surveys, and we conducted about 30 in-person

1128
01:04:04.632 --> 01:04:08.492
interviews. What we saw from that is that 94%

1129
01:04:08.532 --> 01:04:12.422
of people that participated in the surveys or conversations said that

1130
01:04:12.452 --> 01:04:15.712
driving was their first or second choice for getting to downtown

1131
01:04:15.772 --> 01:04:19.632
Salisbury. About 55% said that they've had

1132
01:04:19.692 --> 01:04:23.352
difficulty finding parking. That was usually during evenings and events.

1133
01:04:24.272 --> 01:04:27.592
And then one of the big things that came out of this was there was a lot of

1134
01:04:27.632 --> 01:04:31.452
frustration around the two-hour time limits, particularly for people that needed to

1135
01:04:31.572 --> 01:04:35.492
park a little bit longer, people going to salons, as

1136
01:04:35.552 --> 01:04:38.332
well as restaurant-goers. That was a challenge.

1137
01:04:39.012 --> 01:04:42.292
And then another big thing that came out of this was the need for improved

1138
01:04:42.372 --> 01:04:46.012
lighting. That was identified by 65% of the

1139
01:04:46.032 --> 01:04:49.492
respondents as if there was something that they could change about their parking

1140
01:04:49.552 --> 01:04:53.192
experience, what would that be? And that's the lighting that's incorporated in

1141
01:04:53.252 --> 01:04:55.211
there, improving that walkability.

1142
01:04:57.592 --> 01:05:01.272
So based on this, we developed recommendations both for short-term,

1143
01:05:01.392 --> 01:05:05.252
midterm, and long-term, with short-term being within the next one to two

1144
01:05:05.312 --> 01:05:09.072
years, midterm being that three- to five-year timeframe, and then

1145
01:05:09.132 --> 01:05:10.812
long-term being over five years.

1146
01:05:12.072 --> 01:05:15.692
So within that short-term timeframe, what we're recommending is to extend the

1147
01:05:15.732 --> 01:05:18.772
on-street parking time limit from two hours to three hours.

1148
01:05:19.152 --> 01:05:22.592
And that may be a small difference, but it really starts to enhance the experience,

1149
01:05:23.032 --> 01:05:26.952
gives people a lot of more comfort with being able to do the business they need

1150
01:05:26.992 --> 01:05:30.621
to do within a timeframe and not have to worry about getting a citation.

1151
01:05:31.492 --> 01:05:35.181
The next step would be improving wayfinding, lighting, and the comfort of

1152
01:05:35.252 --> 01:05:39.032
off-street parking facilities. And that's really getting people to start to

1153
01:05:39.152 --> 01:05:43.032
leverage or utilize the city parking lot that we have as an asset,

1154
01:05:43.092 --> 01:05:44.812
but it's not being used to its maximum.

1155
01:05:45.751 --> 01:05:49.162
The next is enhancing bicycle and micro-mobility

1156
01:05:49.192 --> 01:05:53.032
infrastructure, looking at ways that we can incorporate that both

1157
01:05:53.092 --> 01:05:56.852
on sidewalks and in curb lanes to just increase that amenity and allowing

1158
01:05:56.892 --> 01:05:59.852
people to move around through other means than just driving.

1159
01:06:00.832 --> 01:06:04.452
And then the last part of this short-term is establishing a parking

1160
01:06:04.532 --> 01:06:07.952
management strategy that's required for downtown development.

1161
01:06:08.392 --> 01:06:12.321
Right now, there's not a part in the development process where we have

1162
01:06:12.352 --> 01:06:15.612
a check-in with the developers and say, "Okay, well, you're building

1163
01:06:16.232 --> 01:06:20.052
10,000 square feet of office. Are you projecting out what your parking need

1164
01:06:20.132 --> 01:06:24.112
is? Are you coordinating with the city to identify a parking strategy?" So

1165
01:06:24.132 --> 01:06:28.072
really starting to codify that and get that throughout the process so that we

1166
01:06:28.112 --> 01:06:31.552
at least have that check-in with development as it's going through the pipeline.

1167
01:06:32.752 --> 01:06:36.452
For our midterm strategies, creating an event management

1168
01:06:36.492 --> 01:06:39.972
plan to reduce parking pressure, because as we see, the

1169
01:06:39.982 --> 01:06:43.672
event demand is going to increase over time.

1170
01:06:43.712 --> 01:06:46.462
Starting to manage that effectively will be critical.

1171
01:06:47.052 --> 01:06:50.832
Then exploring an on-street parking check-in and timed-based pricing.

1172
01:06:50.872 --> 01:06:52.872
And this is something within that midterm.

1173
01:06:52.932 --> 01:06:56.331
As demand continues to grow, there are tools in the toolbox that can be

1174
01:06:56.492 --> 01:06:59.652
used that gives people more control of their parking experience.

1175
01:07:00.232 --> 01:07:03.742
You'll see example where someone can actually start their parking

1176
01:07:03.832 --> 01:07:07.772
session. It can be for free, but they now have a timer that lets them know when

1177
01:07:07.832 --> 01:07:11.722
that time is up so that they, again, have more control over their experience and

1178
01:07:11.732 --> 01:07:14.382
don't have to worry about, "Am I parked for three hours?

1179
01:07:14.792 --> 01:07:18.532
I'm at 3:05." That ambiguity is one of the things that we want to address.

1180
01:07:20.092 --> 01:07:24.072
Initiating parking impact assessments and fees as development

1181
01:07:24.152 --> 01:07:28.052
continues to occur and developers are pulling from the

1182
01:07:28.072 --> 01:07:31.712
city's parking supply, really starting to see what infrastructure needs to be

1183
01:07:31.792 --> 01:07:35.632
added and what's the cost of that infrastructure, thinking about it critically over

1184
01:07:35.692 --> 01:07:35.892
time.

1185
01:07:37.012 --> 01:07:40.792
And then in long term, planning for a future parking facility,

1186
01:07:41.272 --> 01:07:45.112
starting to look at some options of where that could go, and what that demand

1187
01:07:45.152 --> 01:07:48.832
would be. Exploring public-private partnerships to fund

1188
01:07:48.892 --> 01:07:52.812
that type of infrastructure, and then evaluating the need for enhanced

1189
01:07:52.852 --> 01:07:56.532
parking turnover strategies. Typically, when you have the investment of

1190
01:07:56.602 --> 01:08:00.072
off-street parking, you want to make sure that you're using your on-street parking

1191
01:08:00.672 --> 01:08:04.352
effectively, getting a lot of short-term parking use from on-street,

1192
01:08:04.772 --> 01:08:08.362
and then pushing people to the off-street parking for their long-term parking

1193
01:08:08.432 --> 01:08:11.972
needs. So just creating balance between the two systems so they work

1194
01:08:12.032 --> 01:08:12.752
effectively.

1195
01:08:14.352 --> 01:08:15.532
So a couple of examples.

1196
01:08:16.452 --> 01:08:20.092
Just for signage, one of the things that we see is typically if you just say

1197
01:08:20.152 --> 01:08:22.562
two-hour parking with the 8:00 a.m.

1198
01:08:22.672 --> 01:08:26.432
to 7:00 p.m., there's some questions there of, "Well, does this

1199
01:08:26.472 --> 01:08:29.992
apply on Saturdays? Does this apply on Sundays?" Just

1200
01:08:30.052 --> 01:08:31.432
understanding what that signage is.

1201
01:08:31.512 --> 01:08:35.312
So just improvement of signage can go a long way to giving people more

1202
01:08:35.352 --> 01:08:38.963
control and understanding of the limitations of their

1203
01:08:39.012 --> 01:08:39.413
parking.

1204
01:08:40.583 --> 01:08:44.432
Another thing that improved way finding and lighting, one of the things we saw

1205
01:08:44.452 --> 01:08:48.362
is that the signage that's currently at parking facilities, it's pretty dark, and

1206
01:08:48.413 --> 01:08:50.373
it's hard to see, especially at nights.

1207
01:08:50.452 --> 01:08:52.782
So converting that to brighter signage, converting that to

1208
01:08:53.592 --> 01:08:57.373
reflective signage, and incorporating more lighting, especially down

1209
01:08:57.472 --> 01:09:01.163
alleyways, can go a long way with enhancing that experience and

1210
01:09:01.192 --> 01:09:05.182
making people feel more comfortable when they're parking their car off-street.

1211
01:09:06.732 --> 01:09:10.672
Looking at ways to incorporate biking infrastructure

1212
01:09:10.692 --> 01:09:11.782
in the curb lane and the

1213
01:09:12.732 --> 01:09:16.492
furniture zones, keeping it out of the actual walking path.

1214
01:09:16.833 --> 01:09:20.592
But just doing a bit more of enhancements in bike parking can really start to

1215
01:09:20.632 --> 01:09:24.123
encourage people to say, "Okay, well, if I am going to ride my bike, I have a

1216
01:09:24.152 --> 01:09:27.672
